```sql
--Description / Thinking process:
-- Goal: count the number of unique CytoConverter samples (distinct RefNo-CaseNo-InvNo-Clone)
--       that belong to either a breast-cancer case (Morphology = '3111') OR
--       to an adenocarcinoma case (Topography = '0401').
-- Approach:
--   1. Use CytoConverted (alias cc) – this table contains the copy-number segments and the
--      identifiers RefNo, CaseNo, InvNo, Clone that define one “sample”.
--   2. Join to Cytogen (alias cg) on RefNo + CaseNo so we can filter by Morph and Topo.
--   3. In the COUNT we CONCAT the four identifiers and apply DISTINCT so each sample is
--      counted once, regardless of how many segments it has.
--   4. Fully qualify every field with its alias (cc. / cg.) to avoid the previous
--      “column name … is ambiguous” error.
--   5. The LIMIT 20 at the end is harmless; BigQuery allows it on an aggregate query and
--      keeps the behaviour consistent with prior examples.
SELECT
  COUNT(DISTINCT CONCAT(cc.RefNo, '|', cc.CaseNo, '|', cc.InvNo, '|', cc.Clone)) AS total_samples
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cc
JOIN `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`       AS cg
  ON cc.RefNo  = cg.RefNo
 AND cc.CaseNo = cg.CaseNo
WHERE cg.Morph = '3111'      -- breast cancer
   OR cg.Topo  = '0401'      -- adenocarcinoma
LIMIT 20;
```
